/*

* ============================================================================
* Zamani Universal Computing Language
* ============================================================================
* 
* File:
* grammar/data/tensors.g4
* 
* Grammar:
* DataTensors
* 
* Status:
* PRODUCTION DATA-DOMAIN TENSOR ADAPTER / COMPOSITION LEAF
* 
* Language:
* Zamani Universal Computing Language
* 
* Rust baseline:
* Rust 1.97 / Rust 1.97.1
* Rust edition 2021
* Safe Rust only
* 
* ============================================================================
* PURPOSE
* ============================================================================
* 
* This file defines the DATA-DOMAIN syntax boundary for tensor-valued data.
* 
* It intentionally does NOT define a second tensor language.
* 
* Tensor computation already has a canonical tensor-domain grammar:
* 
* grammar/classical/tensor.g4
* 
* AI tensor-domain syntax is separately composed through:
* 
* grammar/ai/tensors.g4
* 
* General data syntax is composed through:
* 
* grammar/data/data.g4
* 
* This file therefore provides the narrow integration boundary required when
* tensors are treated as DATA:
* 
* - tensor-valued data declarations;
* - tensor data construction references;
* - tensor data shape/value boundaries;
* - tensor data metadata;
* - tensor data transformation hooks;
* - tensor data serialization/materialization hooks;
* - tensor data resource/capability intent hooks.
* 
* Tensor arithmetic, tensor indexing, tensor operation semantics, tensor type
* semantics, broadcasting, contraction, layout, allocation, scheduling,
* optimization, accelerator selection, and hardware realization remain owned
* by their canonical downstream domains.
* 
* ============================================================================
* ARCHITECTURAL POSITION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Zamani source
*      |
*      v
* grammar/antlr/ZamaniLexer.g4
*      |
*      v
* grammar/antlr/ZamaniParser.g4
*      |
*      v
* Data composition
*      |
*      +-----------------------------+
*      |                             |
*      v                             v
* ordinary data syntax         data tensor boundary
*                                    |
*                                    v
*                          canonical Tensor grammar
*                          grammar/classical/tensor.g4
*                                    |
*                                    v
*                              domain-neutral AST
*                                    |
*                                    v
*                            semantic analysis
*                                    |
*                     +--------------+---------------+
*                     |                              |
*                     v                              v
*               data semantics                 tensor semantics
*                     |                              |
*                     +--------------+---------------+
*                                    |
*                                    v
*                           canonical semantic model
*                                    |
*                     +--------------+---------------+
*                     |                              |
*                     v                              v
*               canonical data/compute IR       Classical IR
*                                                    |
*                                                    v
*                                              optimization
*                                                    |
*                                          scheduling/lowering
*                                                    |
*                                                    v
*                                              target runtime
* 
* Quantum consumers remain downstream of the canonical quantum::ir boundary.
* 
* This grammar MUST NOT create another tensor IR or another quantum IR.
* 
* ============================================================================
* OWNERSHIP
* ============================================================================
* 
* THIS FILE OWNS:
* 
* - data-domain tensor composition entry points;
* - tensor-valued data references;
* - tensor-valued data construction boundaries;
* - tensor data shape/value boundaries;
* - tensor data metadata;
* - tensor data transformation hooks;
* - tensor data materialization hooks;
* - tensor data serialization hooks;
* - tensor data requirement/capability hooks;
* - tensor data portability boundaries.
* 
* THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN:
* 
* - lexical rules;
* - lexer tokens;
* - identifiers;
* - qualified-name syntax;
* - general expressions;
* - expression precedence;
* - arithmetic;
* - indexing;
* - slicing;
* - general calls;
* - universal types;
* - tensor type semantics;
* - general declarations;
* - records;
* - schemas;
* - collections;
* - streams;
* - databases;
* - storage engines;
* - networking;
* - memory allocation;
* - hardware;
* - accelerators;
* - GPU/FPGA/QPU selection;
* - scheduling;
* - routing;
* - optimization;
* - automatic differentiation;
* - quantum semantics;
* - quantum::ir;
* - QEC;
* - ZQN;
* - HAL;
* - runtime execution.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SINGLE-SOURCE TENSOR RULE
* ============================================================================
* 
* There MUST be one canonical source-level tensor computation grammar.
* 
* That grammar is:
* 
* grammar/classical/tensor.g4
* 
* This file MUST NOT redefine:
* 
* tensorLiteral
* tensorConstruction
* tensorOperationInvocation
* tensorShapeSpecification
* tensorDimensionList
* tensorAxisList
* tensorDomainExpression
* 
* when those rules are already supplied by the canonical Tensor grammar.
* 
* Instead, this grammar imports and reuses the canonical tensor rules.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SINGLE-SOURCE TYPE RULE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor types remain owned by the canonical type system.
* 
* This file therefore consumes:
* 
* typeExpression
* 
* and MUST NOT define a competing:
* 
* dataTensorType
* tensorType
* tensorTypeExpression
* 
* hierarchy.
* 
* Examples of semantic tensor types include:
* 
* Tensor<T>
* Tensor<T, Shape>
* 
* and other future type-system representations.
* 
* Whether a particular type denotes a tensor is a semantic question.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SINGLE-SOURCE EXPRESSION RULE
* ============================================================================
* 
* General expressions remain owned by the canonical expression grammar.
* 
* This file consumes:
* 
* expression
* 
* and MUST NOT create a second tensor-specific expression precedence system.
* 
* Consequently:
* 
* A + B
* A * B
* f(A)
* A[i]
* A[i, j]
* A[i, j, k]
* 
* remain ordinary Zamani expressions.
* 
* Their tensor semantics are determined after parsing.
* 
* ============================================================================
* OPEN-WORLD OPERATION MODEL
* ============================================================================
* 
* Data tensors may be consumed by arbitrary tensor operations.
* 
* The grammar MUST NOT enumerate a closed operation list such as:
* 
* reshape
* transpose
* matmul
* convolution
* einsum
* reduce
* broadcast
* concatenate
* stack
* svd
* qr
* lu
* 
* Operation names remain extensible source-level names.
* 
* They may resolve to:
* 
* - standard library operations;
* - user-defined operations;
* - mathematical operations;
* - AI operations;
* - accelerator operations;
* - dialect operations;
* - vendor-neutral interoperability operations;
* - future operations.
* 
* Adding a new tensor algorithm MUST NOT require changing this grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
* DATA/TENSOR SEPARATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* A tensor is a computational/data abstraction.
* 
* A data tensor is not a separate physical storage abstraction.
* 
* For example:
* 
* tensor-valued data
* 
* may ultimately be represented in:
* 
* - host memory;
* - accelerator memory;
* - distributed memory;
* - persistent storage;
* - streamed storage;
* - database-backed storage;
* - object storage;
* - generated hardware;
* - another future representation.
* 
* This file describes the logical data relationship only.
* 
* ============================================================================
* POCO-REAF CONTRACT
* ============================================================================
* 
* This grammar participates in:
* 
* Program_Once_Compile_Once_Run_Everywhere_Anywhere_Forever
* 
* Tensor-valued data syntax MUST therefore remain independent of:
* 
* - CPU model;
* - CPU count;
* - core count;
* - thread count;
* - GPU model;
* - GPU count;
* - FPGA model;
* - FPGA count;
* - accelerator model;
* - QPU model;
* - device count;
* - node count;
* - memory capacity;
* - storage capacity;
* - register width;
* - SIMD width;
* - vector width;
* - network topology;
* - physical address;
* - vendor library;
* - database provider;
* - cloud provider.
* 
* A logical tensor data declaration must remain meaningful when the available
* target resources change.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SCALABILITY CONTRACT
* ============================================================================
* 
* This grammar imposes NO language-level maximum on:
* 
* - tensor rank;
* - tensor dimensions;
* - tensor elements;
* - tensor declarations;
* - tensor values;
* - tensor metadata;
* - tensor arguments;
* - tensor operations;
* - nested tensor structures;
* - datasets;
* - records;
* - collections;
* - streams;
* - partitions;
* - replicas;
* - distributed participants;
* - devices;
* - memory capacity.
* 
* In particular, this grammar MUST NOT define:
* 
* MAX_TENSOR_RANK
* MAX_TENSOR_DIMENSIONS
* MAX_TENSOR_ELEMENTS
* MAX_TENSOR_SIZE
* MAX_TENSOR_COUNT
* MAX_TENSOR_ARGUMENTS
* MAX_DATASET_SIZE
* MAX_PARTITIONS
* MAX_REPLICAS
* MAX_NODES
* MAX_MEMORY
* MAX_DEVICES
* 
* Repetition is represented structurally using ANTLR repetition operators.
* 
* Practical implementation limits are environmental or resource constraints,
* not language semantics.
* 
* ============================================================================
* HARD-CODING PROHIBITION
* ============================================================================
* 
* No universal hardware limit may be encoded in this grammar.
* 
* Forbidden examples include:
* 
* MAX_QUBITS
* MAX_CPUS
* MAX_GPUS
* MAX_FPGAS
* MAX_NODES
* MAX_MEMORY
* MAX_THREADS
* MAX_TENSOR_RANK
* MAX_REGISTER_WIDTH
* MAX_NETWORK_SIZE
* MAX_DEVICE_COUNT
* 
* The following distinction MUST remain intact:
* 
* let n = 1024;
* 
* is program data.
* 
* It does NOT establish:
* 
* tensor dimensions <= 1024
* 
* as a language rule.
* 
* Likewise:
* 
* requires memory >= required_memory
* 
* expresses a resource requirement.
* 
* It does NOT define a universal memory capacity.
* 
* ============================================================================
* RESOURCE / CAPABILITY SEPARATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may require semantic capabilities such as:
* 
* requires capability("tensor.compute")
* requires capability("tensor.reshape")
* requires capability("tensor.distributed")
* requires capability("tensor.persistence")
* 
* Such expressions are source-level intent.
* 
* They do not select:
* 
* GPU 0
* CPU 3
* FPGA 1
* QPU 2
* node 17
* 
* Physical realization belongs downstream.
* 
* ============================================================================
* REQUIREMENT / CONSTRAINT / PREFERENCE / HINT
* ============================================================================
* 
* These concepts MUST remain semantically distinct.
* 
* REQUIREMENT:
* 
* execution cannot satisfy the program's semantics without the property.
* 
* CONSTRAINT:
* 
* a valid implementation must not violate the property.
* 
* PREFERENCE:
* 
* an implementation satisfying the preference is preferred when possible.
* 
* HINT:
* 
* information supplied to optimization without changing program meaning.
* 
* IMPLEMENTATION DECISION:
* 
* a downstream target-specific realization.
* 
* This grammar only exposes source syntax.
* 
* Semantic analysis decides the classification.
* 
* ============================================================================
* AST CONTRACT
* ============================================================================
* 
* The parser must produce ordinary domain-neutral frontend structures.
* 
* Conceptual mapping:
* 
* dataTensorConstruct
*         |
*         v
* generic data/tensor AST
*         |
*         v
* semantic tensor-data model
*         |
*         v
* canonical data/compute representation
* 
* No TensorDataIR is created by this grammar.
* 
* No DataTensorIR is created by this grammar.
* 
* No TensorHardwareIR is created by this grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SEMANTIC CONTRACT
* ============================================================================
* 
* Semantic analysis is responsible for determining:
* 
* - whether an expression is tensor-valued;
* - element type;
* - rank;
* - dimension relationships;
* - shape compatibility;
* - broadcasting validity;
* - indexing validity;
* - operation compatibility;
* - ownership;
* - mutability;
* - lifetime;
* - resource requirements;
* - capability requirements;
* - serialization compatibility;
* - materialization requirements;
* - distributed data constraints;
* - security/privacy constraints.
* 
* The grammar performs none of these semantic checks.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SHAPE SEMANTICS
* ============================================================================
* 
* Shape values may be:
* 
* - constants;
* - variables;
* - symbolic values;
* - expressions;
* - dependent values;
* - runtime values where the type system permits them.
* 
* The grammar must not require shape dimensions to be machine constants.
* 
* Example:
* 
* shape = <batch, height, width, channels>
* 
* is valid semantic intent when the surrounding language permits it.
* 
* The grammar does not decide whether those values are statically known.
* 
* ============================================================================
* DATA MATERIALIZATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may be logical or materialized.
* 
* The distinction belongs to semantic/data execution layers.
* 
* The grammar may expose a materialization boundary without selecting:
* 
* RAM
* VRAM
* storage device
* database
* distributed store
* accelerator memory
* 
* ============================================================================
* SERIALIZATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor serialization is a data concern.
* 
* This grammar may express serialization intent through a generic data
* serialization boundary.
* 
* It MUST NOT implement or enumerate a closed list of serialization engines.
* 
* A format identifier remains extensible semantic data.
* 
* ============================================================================
* DISTRIBUTED TENSOR DATA
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may be partitioned, replicated, streamed, or distributed.
* 
* This grammar may expose logical distribution metadata.
* 
* It MUST NOT encode a fixed physical number of:
* 
* partitions;
* replicas;
* nodes;
* devices;
* links.
* 
* For example:
* 
* partition by expression
* 
* is semantic intent.
* 
* It does not mean:
* 
* partition into N physical partitions.
* 
* ============================================================================
* QUANTUM INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may coexist with quantum computation.
* 
* This file does NOT define quantum syntax.
* 
* Quantum syntax remains owned by:
* 
* grammar/quantum/
* 
* The canonical quantum semantic boundary remains:
* 
* quantum::ir
* 
* Tensor data may represent:
* 
* - measurement data;
* - classical parameters;
* - observables;
* - experiment results;
* - model parameters;
* - classical feed-forward data.
* 
* Quantum operation semantics remain outside this grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
* AI INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* AI/ML tensor semantics remain owned by:
* 
* grammar/ai/
* 
* This file permits tensor-valued data to cross the data/AI boundary without
* making any AI framework a language primitive.
* 
* It MUST NOT enumerate:
* 
* PyTorch
* TensorFlow
* JAX
* CUDA
* ROCm
* 
* or other framework/vendor names as required tensor syntax.
* 
* ============================================================================
* CLASSICAL INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor computation remains owned by:
* 
* grammar/classical/tensor.g4
* 
* This grammar reuses that canonical tensor construct boundary rather than
* redefining tensor operations.
* 
* ============================================================================
* COLLECTION INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Collections may contain tensors.
* 
* The collection grammar remains the authority for collection syntax.
* 
* This file must not define:
* 
* tensorCollection
* 
* as a competing collection type system.
* 
* A collection's element type may semantically be tensor-valued.
* 
* ============================================================================
* RECORD INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Records may contain tensor-valued fields.
* 
* Record declaration syntax remains owned by:
* 
* grammar/declarations/records.g4
* 
* Data record value syntax remains owned by:
* 
* grammar/data/
* 
* This file only provides tensor-specific data classification hooks.
* 
* ============================================================================
* STREAM INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Streams may carry tensor values.
* 
* Stream declaration and stream operators remain owned by:
* 
* grammar/data/streams.g4
* 
* This file does not redefine stream syntax.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SCHEMA INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Schemas may describe tensor-valued fields.
* 
* Schema declaration syntax remains owned by the schema grammar.
* 
* Tensor shape constraints are semantic constraints and do not become a
* duplicate schema grammar here.
* 
* ============================================================================
* NETWORKING INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may cross logical network boundaries.
* 
* Networking owns:
* 
* endpoints;
* protocols;
* routing;
* transport;
* network topology.
* 
* This grammar owns none of those implementation details.
* 
* ============================================================================
* MEMORY INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor values may eventually occupy one or more memory domains.
* 
* Memory ownership, allocation, address spaces, borrowing, persistence,
* placement, and physical memory selection remain outside this grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
* HARDWARE INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor data may eventually be lowered to:
* 
* CPU
* GPU
* FPGA
* ASIC
* accelerator
* distributed target
* future compute architecture
* 
* This grammar must remain unchanged when the target changes.
* 
* ============================================================================
* COMPILER INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* The compiler consumes semantic tensor/data representations.
* 
* It must not consume this grammar directly as an execution specification.
* 
* Conceptual path:
* 
* parser context
*      |
*      v
* domain-neutral AST
*      |
*      v
* semantic tensor/data model
*      |
*      v
* canonical IR
*      |
*      v
* optimization
*      |
*      v
* resource-aware lowering
*      |
*      v
* scheduling
*      |
*      v
* target realization
* 
* Adding a new tensor backend must not require changing this grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
* RUNTIME INTEGRATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Runtime systems may discover:
* 
* memory;
* devices;
* accelerators;
* network resources;
* storage;
* execution capabilities.
* 
* Runtime discovery MUST NOT alter parser semantics.
* 
* ============================================================================
* DETERMINISM
* ============================================================================
* 
* Parsing MUST depend only on:
* 
* source;
* token stream;
* language/grammar version.
* 
* Parsing MUST NOT depend on:
* 
* available hardware;
* memory capacity;
* CPU count;
* GPU availability;
* network state;
* filesystem state;
* runtime state;
* randomness;
* wall-clock time.
* 
* ============================================================================
* SAFETY
* ============================================================================
* 
* This grammar contains:
* 
* - no embedded Rust actions;
* - no semantic predicates;
* - no filesystem access;
* - no network access;
* - no process execution;
* - no environment inspection;
* - no hardware discovery;
* - no runtime execution;
* - no unsafe code.
* 
* Rust 1.97 / 1.97.1 compatibility is enforced by the downstream Rust
* implementation and build/conformance system.
* 
* ============================================================================
* LEXICAL CONTRACT
* ============================================================================
* 
* This is a parser grammar.
* 
* It MUST consume the canonical:
* 
* ZamaniLexer
* 
* token vocabulary.
* 
* It MUST NOT declare lexer rules.
* 
* Tensor operation names are intentionally not keywords.
* 
* ============================================================================
* GRAMMAR DECLARATION
* ============================================================================
  */

parser grammar DataTensors;

options {
tokenVocab = ZamaniLexer;
}

import Tensor, Types, Expressions;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 1. PUBLIC DATA-TENSOR ENTRY POINT
* ============================================================================
* 
* This is the only public entry point owned by this file.
* 
* It intentionally describes data-domain tensor structure rather than
* reimplementing tensor computation.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorConstruct
: dataTensorValue
| dataTensorBinding
| dataTensorShape
| dataTensorMetadata
| dataTensorTransform
| dataTensorMaterialization
| dataTensorSerialization
| dataTensorRequirement
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 2. TENSOR DATA VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* A tensor data value may be:
* 
* - a canonical tensor construct;
* - an ordinary Zamani expression whose semantic type is tensor-valued.
* 
* The first alternative provides an explicit tensor-domain parse boundary.
* The second preserves ordinary expression semantics.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorValue
: tensorConstruct
| expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 3. TENSOR DATA BINDING
* ============================================================================
* 
* This is a data-domain binding boundary.
* 
* It does not replace the universal declaration grammar.
* 
* It is intended for data-domain consumers that need to identify a logical
* tensor binding before semantic lowering.
* 
* Example:
* 
* tensor_data A: Tensor<f64> = value
* 
* Whether "tensor_data" is exposed as stable source syntax is controlled by
* the canonical data specification/dispatcher.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorBinding
: TENSOR_DATA
identifier
COLON
typeExpression
dataTensorInitializer?
SEMICOLON
;

dataTensorInitializer
: ASSIGN
expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 4. TENSOR DATA SHAPE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses the canonical Tensor grammar's shape specification.
* 
* No rank or dimension maximum exists.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorShape
: TENSOR_SHAPE
tensorShapeSpecification
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 5. TENSOR DATA METADATA
* ============================================================================
* 
* Metadata is intentionally open-ended.
* 
* The key is a canonical identifier.
* 
* The value is a canonical expression.
* 
* Semantic validation determines whether a metadata key is recognized.
* 
* This avoids continuously extending the grammar for every future tensor
* metadata concept.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorMetadata
: TENSOR_METADATA
LBRACE
dataTensorMetadataEntry*
RBRACE
;

dataTensorMetadataEntry
: identifier
COLON
expression
COMMA?
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 6. TENSOR DATA TRANSFORMATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Tensor transformation names are open-world.
* 
* Examples include:
* 
* reshape
* transpose
* cast
* normalize
* standardize
* quantize
* dequantize
* custom_operation
* 
* These are NOT grammar keywords.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorTransform
: TENSOR_TRANSFORM
qualifiedName
LPAREN
dataTensorArgumentList?
RPAREN
;

dataTensorArgumentList
: dataTensorArgument
(COMMA dataTensorArgument)*
COMMA?
;

dataTensorArgument
: identifier
COLON
expression
| expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 7. MATERIALIZATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Materialization is logical data intent.
* 
* It does not select physical memory or storage.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorMaterialization
: TENSOR_MATERIALIZE
expression
dataTensorMaterializationOption*
SEMICOLON
;

dataTensorMaterializationOption
: identifier
(ASSIGN expression)?
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 8. SERIALIZATION
* ============================================================================
* 
* Serialization format names remain open-world identifiers.
* 
* This grammar does not enumerate serialization technologies.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorSerialization
: TENSOR_SERIALIZE
expression
dataTensorSerializationFormat?
SEMICOLON
;

dataTensorSerializationFormat
: identifier
| STRING
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 9. RESOURCE / CAPABILITY REQUIREMENT
* ============================================================================
* 
* This rule provides a tensor-data-specific classification boundary.
* 
* Resource validation remains downstream.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorRequirement
: REQUIRES
expression
SEMICOLON
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 10. TENSOR VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Stable wrapper for consumers that need a tensor-valued data boundary.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensor
: expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 11. TENSOR TYPE
* ============================================================================
* 
* This rule is deliberately only an adapter to the canonical type system.
* 
* It does not establish a competing tensor type grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorType
: typeExpression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 12. TENSOR SHAPE VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Shape values remain ordinary expressions.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorShapeValue
: expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 13. TENSOR AXIS VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Axis values remain ordinary expressions.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorAxisValue
: expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 14. TENSOR OPERATION VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* This adapter exposes the canonical tensor operation boundary to data-domain
* consumers without enumerating operations.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorOperation
: tensorOperationInvocation
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 15. TENSOR CONSTRUCTION VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses the canonical tensor construction grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorConstruction
: tensorConstruction
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 16. TENSOR LITERAL VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses the canonical tensor literal grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorLiteral
: tensorLiteral
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 17. TENSOR SHAPE SPECIFICATION VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses canonical tensor shape syntax.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorShapeSpecification
: tensorShapeSpecification
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 18. TENSOR DIMENSION LIST VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses the canonical tensor dimension-list grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorDimensionList
: tensorDimensionList
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 19. TENSOR AXIS LIST VALUE
* ============================================================================
* 
* Reuses the canonical tensor axis-list grammar.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorAxisList
: tensorAxisList
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 20. SEMANTIC DATA-TENSOR ARGUMENT
* ============================================================================
* 
* An argument may be:
* 
* - named;
* - positional.
* 
* The expression grammar remains authoritative.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorSemanticArgument
: identifier
COLON
expression
| expression
;

dataTensorSemanticArgumentList
: dataTensorSemanticArgument
(COMMA dataTensorSemanticArgument)*
COMMA?
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 21. TENSOR DATA CONSTRUCT LIST
* ============================================================================
* 
* Unbounded repetition is intentional.
* 
* No fixed number of tensor data constructs is imposed.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorConstructList
: dataTensorConstruct*
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 22. DATA/TENSOR EXPRESSION ADAPTER
* ============================================================================
* 
* This adapter exists for data-domain composition grammars.
* 
* It does not create a second expression hierarchy.
* 
* ============================================================================
  */

dataTensorExpression
: expression
;

/*

* ============================================================================
* 23. DATA/TENSOR INITIALIZER ADAPTER
* ============================================================================
